{
  "id": "Compound::DB00310 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Chlorthalidone is linked to Hypertension (disease) through a series of pharmacological pathways that converge on the class of diuretics. The evidence suggests that Chlorthalidone, through its inclusion in increased diuresis, is associated with Metolazone, Indapamide, and Triamterene, all of which are diuretics. These diuretics are known to reduce blood volume and pressure, contributing to the treatment of hypertension. The shared lowest common ancestors—Thiazide Diuretic and Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compound—highlight a common pharmacological profile, reinforcing the idea that Chlorthalidone may exert its effects through a mechanism involving diuretic action. The integration of these pathways underscores the potential for Chlorthalidone to be repurposed for hypertension treatment, based on its pharmacological relationship with diuretics and its role in increasing diuresis.",
